1️⃣ WHY INTERFACES EXIST
===============================================================================
💡 Problem:
-----------
In testbenches, DUTs (Design Under Test) often have many ports:
    clk, rst, addr, data, valid, ready, etc.

Without interfaces, every module or TB component must connect these one by one.
This becomes messy, error-prone, and hard to maintain.

💡 Solution:
------------
SystemVerilog’s `interface` groups related signals into a single bundle.
Now you can connect **one interface instance** instead of 10+ separate signals.

It also allows:
✅ Centralized signal definition  
✅ Cleaner connections  
✅ Easier reuse across testbench components  
✅ Additional features like clocking blocks, modports, and built-in tasks.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example (Basic Interface)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
interface dut_if(input logic clk);
  logic rst, a, b, y;
endinterface

module tb;
  logic clk;
  dut_if i_if(clk);          // interface instance
  dut d1(.if_port(i_if));    // connect DUT
  driver drv(i_if);          // connect TB component
  monitor mon(i_if);
endmodule

🧠 All signals (rst, a, b, y) are bundled inside `i_if`.

===============================================================================
2️⃣ INTERFACE VS DUT VS TESTBENCH
===============================================================================
📘 Clarification of Roles:
--------------------------
- DUT → Design Under Test (the hardware module you are verifying)
- Testbench → Verification environment (stimulus, checking, reporting)
- Interface → Shared bundle that connects DUT ↔ Testbench

So, TB and DUT are *different entities* running concurrently in simulation, communicating through signals grouped inside an interface.

