4️⃣ INTERFACE PORTS
===============================================================================
Interfaces themselves can have **ports** — inputs, outputs, etc.
This allows one interface to connect to another or receive external signals like clocks or resets.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
interface mem_if (
  input  wire         clk,
  input  wire         reset,
  input  wire         we,
  input  wire         ce,
  input  wire   [7:0] datai,
  output logic  [7:0] datao,
  input  wire   [7:0] addr
);
endinterface

module simple_if (mem_if mif);
  logic [7:0] mem [0:255];

  always @(posedge mif.clk)
    if (mif.reset) mif.datao <= 0;
    else if (mif.ce && !mif.we)
      mif.datao <= mem[mif.addr];

  always @(posedge mif.clk)
    if (mif.ce && mif.we)
      mem[mif.addr] <= mif.datai;
endmodule

🧠 Why Use Interface Ports?
→ To cleanly connect signals from external modules (like clk, reset) directly to the interface, keeping testbench code simpler.

===============================================================================
5️⃣ MODPORTS
===============================================================================
🧩 Purpose:
------------
By default, all signals in an interface are `inout`.
That means any connected module can drive or read them → dangerous!

💡 `modport` fixes this by defining *who can drive or read what*, depending on the module’s role.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
interface bus_if(input logic clk);
  logic [7:0] addr, data;
  logic valid, ready, wr_en, rd_en;

  // Driver’s view (testbench)
  modport drv_mp (output addr, data, wr_en, rd_en, valid,
                  input ready, clk);

  // Monitor’s view (read-only)
  modport mon_mp (input addr, data, wr_en, rd_en, valid, ready, clk);

  // DUT’s view (hardware)
  modport dut_mp  (input addr, data, wr_en, rd_en, valid, clk,
                   output ready);
endinterface

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Clarification of Directions:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each modport defines directions **from the connected module’s point of view.**

| Modport | Module Type | Drives | Reads |
|----------|--------------|--------|-------|
| drv_mp   | Driver (TB)  | addr, data, wr_en, rd_en, valid | ready |
| mon_mp   | Monitor (TB) | — (read-only) | All |
| dut_mp   | DUT (Design) | ready | addr, data, wr_en, rd_en, valid |

💡 This prevents multiple drivers and defines clear ownership of signals.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Analogy:
--------
Think of the interface as a shared room.
Modports are like keys — they decide which doors (signals) each module can open (drive) or only look through (read).

