6️⃣ TASKS AND FUNCTIONS INSIDE INTERFACES
===============================================================================
🧩 Purpose:
------------
Tasks/functions inside interfaces make your testbench **modular and reusable**.
You can write behavior that directly drives signals — acting as a **Bus Functional Model (BFM)**.

💡 These can include timing (`@posedge clk`) and signal drives — something normal class functions cannot do directly.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
interface task_function_if (input clk);
  logic req, gnt;

  // Clocking block for TB
  clocking cb @ (posedge clk);
    input  gnt;
    output req;
  endclocking

  // Task to drive signal with delay
  task drive_req (input integer delay, input bit value);
    $display("@%0dns Before driving req %b", $time, req);
    repeat (delay) @ (posedge clk);
    req = value;
    $display("@%0dns Driving req %b", $time, req);
  endtask

  // Modports
  modport dut (input clk, req, output gnt);
  modport tb  (clocking cb, input clk, import drive_req);
endinterface

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DUT Module:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
module dut_ex (interface abc);
  always @(posedge abc.clk)
    abc.gnt <= abc.req;
endmodule

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TB Module:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
module tb_ex (interface xyz);
  initial begin
    xyz.cb.req <= 0;
    xyz.drive_req(10,1);
    xyz.drive_req(10,0);
  end
endmodule

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Top-Level Connection:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
module interface_task_function();
  logic clk = 0;
  always #1 clk++;

  task_function_if tfif(clk);
  dut_ex U_dut(tfif.dut);
  tb_ex  U_tb(tfif.tb);
endmodule

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Simulation Output (Simplified):
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@0ns Before driving req x
@19ns Driving req 1
@39ns Driving req 0

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧠 What’s Happening:
- TB calls `xyz.drive_req()` (imported task from interface)
- Task drives `req` signal after given delay
- DUT simply echoes `req` → `gnt`
- Everything is synchronized via interface clock

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DV Relevance:
-------------
✅ Used to build BFMs (Bus Functional Models)
✅ Centralizes signal drive logic
✅ Avoids duplicating same sequences in drivers
✅ Foundation concept for UVM drivers using `vif.drive_task(...)`

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Key Terms:
-----------
- `import` → allows module to call task defined inside interface
- `export` → allows interface to call task defined inside module

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Interview Tip:
---------------
Q: Why use tasks/functions in interfaces?
A: They help create reusable, timing-accurate BFMs that directly control DUT signals, improving testbench modularity.

